"[2412.20704] HFI: A unified framework for training-free detection and implicit watermarking of latent diffusion model generated images (https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.20704)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.20704\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 30 Dec 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 13 Apr 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:HFI: A unified framework for training-free detection and implicit watermarking of latent diffusion model generated images\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Sungik Choi , cite7†Hankook Lee , cite8†Jaehoon Lee , cite9†Seunghyun Kim , cite10†Stanley Jungkyu Choi , cite11†Moontae Lee L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled HFI: A unified framework for training-free detection and implicit watermarking of latent diffusion model generated images, by Sungik Choi and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Dramatic advances in the quality of the latent diffusion models (LDMs) also led to the malicious use of AI-generated images. While current AI-generated image detection methods assume the availability of real/AI-generated images for training, this is practically limited given the vast expressibility of LDMs. This motivates the training-free detection setup where no related data are available in advance.\nL17: The existing LDM-generated image detection method assumes that images generated by LDM are easier to reconstruct using an autoencoder than real images. However, we observe that this reconstruction distance is overfitted to background information, leading the current method to underperform in detecting images with simple backgrounds. To address this, we propose a novel method called HFI.\nL18: Specifically, by viewing the autoencoder of LDM as a downsampling-upsampling kernel, HFI measures the extent of aliasing, a distortion of high-frequency information that appears in the reconstructed image. HFI is training-free, efficient, and consistently outperforms other training-free methods in detecting challenging images generated by various generative models. We also show that HFI can successfully detect the images generated from the specified LDM as a means of implicit watermarking.\nL19: HFI outperforms the best baseline method while achieving magnitudes of\nL20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2412.20704 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2412.20704v2 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.20704†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Sungik Choi [cite17†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Mon, 30 Dec 2024 04:34:42 UTC (13,463 KB)\nL28: [v2] Mon, 13 Apr 2026 03:24:39 UTC (13,463 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled HFI: A unified framework for training-free detection and implicit watermarking of latent diffusion model generated images, by Sungik Choi and 5 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite12†View PDF L37:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L46: \nL47: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2024-12 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite25†cs L52: cite26†cs.LG L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.ed